# UBSan runtime suppressions for the provizio_dds sanitizer builds.
# Applied via UBSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=<this file> (see the sanitizer CI job).
#
# Fast-DDS compares serialized payloads with memcmp() even when the payload is empty (data == nullptr,
# length == 0). memcmp is declared __attribute__((nonnull)), so passing the null (but unused, length 0)
# pointer trips UBSan's nonnull-attribute check. It is benign (no bytes are read) and lives entirely in
# Fast-DDS's discovery serialization, so suppress it rather than carry a patch in the fork.
nonnull-attribute:SerializedPayload.cpp

# Fast-DDS represents an infinite timer interval (c_TimeInfinite) as a floating-point +inf and casts it
# to a long long count when arming the event; inf->long long is float-cast-overflow UB. It is benign —
# the value denotes "never fire" — and lives entirely in Fast-DDS's TimedEvent resource. It surfaces
# only on macOS (the Linux runners do not reach this conversion). Suppress it: besides being a Fast-DDS
# internal issue, leaving it unsuppressed makes UBSan symbolize a stack trace on every periodic firing,
# which on macOS is slow enough to stall the timed-event thread and break pub/sub delivery timing.
float-cast-overflow:TimedEvent.cpp

# Fast-DDS' DomainParticipantImpl::disable() calls set_listener(nullptr, <default timeout>) to drain
# in-flight listener callbacks, and that default is on the order of std::chrono::seconds::max().
# libstdc++ 9 implements condition_variable::wait_until(steady_clock) by converting the deadline
# through system_clock (system_clock::now() + (deadline - steady_clock::now())), so adding ~9.2e18
# nanoseconds to system_clock's epoch-based ~1.78e18 overflows int64. Benign — the value means "wait
# indefinitely" — and confined to Fast-DDS: it is reached from every participant destruction, so it can
# surface in any test. Only libstdc++ 9 converts via system_clock (11+ has a dedicated steady_clock
# path), which is why it appears solely on the gcc-9 jetson-20.04 Debug jobs.
#
# Suppressed for the same reason as the TimedEvent entry above: leaving it unsuppressed makes UBSan
# symbolize a 25-frame stack across libfastdds.so, and on a slow aarch64 runner that stall is enough to
# break pub/sub delivery timing (observed as a reliable_pub_sub ctest timeout whose baseline is ~6.5s).
#
# Scoped to the MODULE, not to `chrono`, deliberately: the overflow is located in a <chrono> header but
# compiled into libfastdds.so, and a `signed-integer-overflow:chrono` entry would also mask a genuine
# overflow in provizio_dds' own chrono arithmetic (the network-recovery coalescer does deadline maths).
# Substring matching makes this cover the versioned SONAME (libfastdds.so.3.6) too.
#
# The cost of the module scope: this hides EVERY signed-integer overflow anywhere in libfastdds.so,
# not just the set_listener one — if a future Fast-DDS upgrade introduces a genuine overflow in that
# library, the sanitizer jobs will no longer catch it. Re-evaluate this entry on Fast-DDS bumps.
signed-integer-overflow:libfastdds.so
